From e324024434bdeb81f4c530e25a03ec3259ab4757 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oleksii Zakharov <contact@zalexdev.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 15:58:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] um: do not test the seccomp filter through a libc wrapper

The probe issued sleep(0) to trip its own filter. What libc does with that
is libc's business: bionic answers a zero timeout in userspace and never
enters the kernel, so nothing ever reached the filter and check_seccomp()
reported a working seccomp implementation as "filter did not trap". UML
then fell back to ptrace, which costs about four times as much per guest
syscall -- measured on the phone at 13.6 us/call under SECCOMP against 22
us under ptrace.

glibc happens to make the call, which is the only reason this ever worked.
A probe for whether a filter traps has no business going through a libc
wrapper; close_range above is already issued raw for the same reason.

Verified on an Android phone (4.19 host): the bionic build now reports
"Userspace mode: SECCOMP" where it previously reported ptrace, and the
glibc build is unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Oleksii Zakharov <contact@zalexdev.com>
---
 arch/um/os-Linux/start_up.c | 23 ++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/um/os-Linux/start_up.c b/arch/um/os-Linux/start_up.c
index 0f8eb3ba0cae6..ee6d13d9964ab 100644
--- a/arch/um/os-Linux/start_up.c
+++ b/arch/um/os-Linux/start_up.c
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 #include <sys/stat.h>
 #include <sys/wait.h>
 #include <sys/time.h>
+#include <time.h>
 #include <sys/resource.h>
 #include <asm/unistd.h>
 #include <init.h>
@@ -547,7 +548,27 @@ static int __init seccomp_helper(void *data)
 			SECCOMP_FILTER_FLAG_TSYNC, &prog) != 0)
 		exit(3);
 
-	sleep(0);
+	/*
+	 * Issue the filtered call directly rather than through libc.
+	 *
+	 * This was sleep(0), and what libc does with that is libc's business:
+	 * bionic answers a zero timeout from userspace and never enters the
+	 * kernel, so nothing reached the filter and the probe concluded the host
+	 * could not trap -- reporting a working seccomp implementation as
+	 * missing and dropping UML onto the ptrace path, which costs about four
+	 * times as much per guest syscall. glibc happens to make the call, which
+	 * is the only reason this ever worked.
+	 *
+	 * A probe for "does this filter trap" has no business going through a
+	 * libc wrapper at all; close_range above is already issued raw for the
+	 * same reason.
+	 */
+	{
+		struct timespec ts = { 0, 0 };
+
+		stub_syscall4(__NR_clock_nanosleep, CLOCK_REALTIME, 0,
+			      (long) &ts, 0);
+	}
 
 	/* Never reached. */
 	_exit(4);
